{"step_index":36,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-11-18T05:29:42Z","input_tokens":5471,"cache_read_tokens":83084,"output_tokens":7255,"thinking":"The rule from the board explicitly forbids `read_file` for binary media types. This is a critical constraint for all subsequent operations. Any attempt to use `read_file` on image, video, or audio files must be avoided.\n\n\nThis diagnostic output details speed and Doppler shifts across several camera angles and shots. Each shot shows distinct acceleration or deceleration profiles, correlating visual details with speed changes. The data reveals critical details about vehicle dynamics, potentially useful for analysis.\n\n\nAnalyzing the data shows distinct speed changes and Doppler shifts across different camera angles and shots. High-speed pass-bys and braking zones are clearly indicated by the speed profiles. This detailed kinematic information strongly correlates with visual events, supporting dynamic analysis of vehicle movements.\n\n\nInitial observation: distance attenuation models must account for focal length and microphone focus for telephoto shots, preventing unrealistic silence at long distances. A naive point-source attenuation model would cause telephoto shots like `S20_StoweTele` to sound completely silent when the vehicle is far away. This is because telephoto shots, as used in broadcast, mix parabolic/trackside microphones, resulting in sound levels that do not reflect this.\n\n\nThe analysis reveals graceful curve scaling across various shot types, from close pass-bys to long telephoto views, integrating distance and Doppler effects. Examination of specific camera shots demonstrates observed distance and Doppler shifts correlated with vehicle speed changes, validating the model. The target is a realistic and exciting synthesis, avoiding artifacts, with optimized execution.\n\n\nPrecise sample grid mapping is verified, where each video frame directly corresponds to 2000 audio samples, given the 48kHz sample rate and 24fps video. This ensures that a single audio sample maps to a single sample index, guaranteeing perfect time alignment between video frames and their corresponding audio data. Frame index, sample index, and simulation time calculations are now synchronized.\n\n\nAnalysis of the timing variables confirms the correct mapping between simulation time and video frame index, accounting for the sample rate and frame rate. The time calculations ensure precise alignment, down to the sample level, for the entire video sequence. Examining the speed range reveals that it meets the requirements outlined, confirming that it covers the realistic 8-speed F1 gearbox parameters in the specifications.\n\n\nAnalyzing audio features such as turbo whistle, upshift ignition-cut pop, and downshift rev-match blips is underway. Exact gear ratios for an 8-speed F1 gearbox are being calculated, accounting for a 15,000 RPM limit and realistic shift points. Gear ratios are being modeled geometrically.\n\n\nAnalysis focuses on gearbox ratio calculations for an 8-speed F1 gearbox with a 15,000 RPM limit. Gear ratios are determined to maintain RPM within a target range across shifts, ensuring that the RPM after an upshift stays at or above a minimum threshold (9800 RPM). A geometric progression of ratios is being investigated to satisfy the RPM constraints. Initial ratios are being checked by calculating gear speeds for each gear and shift points, starting with Gear 1 at 88.0 km/h and RPM = 12,550.\n\n\nCalculations now cover all gear ratios and shift points, from Gear 1 through Gear 8. Each upshift is calculated to ensure the post-shift RPM remains above the minimum threshold. Analysis of speed and RPM data reveals consistent upshift RPM targets across gears. The gearbox model delivers consistent performance in the target speed range.\n\n\nHysteresis will be introduced to prevent rapid up/down shifting, with a 5.5 km/h difference between upshift and downshift thresholds, plus a 0.14 s minimum dwell time. This models aggressive braking zone downshifts realistically, around 6–7 shifts/sec.\n\nUpshift transient behavior models RPM and amplitude changes: over 45 ms, RPM smoothly decreases, accompanied by a 55% dip in fundamental amplitude for ~28 ms, and a transient bark/pop (bandpass filtered 180–900 Hz). Downshifts during braking include a 45 ms RPM increase and a 55 ms electronic rev-match blip (+350 RPM).\n\n\nAnalyzing engine firing frequency and sub-orders reveals the core of realistic F1 sound. The 6-cylinder 4-stroke engine fires three times per crankshaft revolution, producing a fundamental frequency (f0) proportional to RPM. Crucially, the model must incorporate half-orders (0.5x, 1.0x, 1.5x, etc.) to capture the asymmetrical nature of the engine bank firing. This approach is essential for avoiding the overly simplistic \"pure-sine buzz\" of amateur synthesizers.\n\n\nThe V6 engine model now incorporates sub-harmonics like 0.5 * f0 (3-cylinder bank order) and 0.333 * f0 (crankshaft 1st order) to achieve a deep, resonant sound. Analysis reveals that these sub-harmonics are essential, as relying solely on f0 leads to a thin sound.\n\nFurther realism will be added by introducing cycle-to-cycle combustion jitter, modeled as filtered stochastic frequency modulation (FM) and amplitude modulation (AM) to simulate the variability of real combustion. The jitter will modulate the frequency (0.25% FM jitter) and amplitude (6% AM jitter) within specific bands (30–250 Hz), breaking the sterile quality of a perfectly repeating digital model.\n\n\nAnalysis now incorporates overrun effects during braking (`throttle < 0.15`) using cylinder deactivation and wastegate bypass to increase exhaust roughness. A harmonic series is being constructed based on crankshaft phase, incorporating harmonics of crank rotation. The fundamental V6 firing frequency ($f_0$) and its harmonics, including half-order sub-harmonics, are used.\n\n\nExhaust simulation integrates asymmetric tube/exhaust saturation and modulated combustion air-pulse noise for realistic chuffing sounds. A Multi-Formant Exhaust Resonator Bank uses bandpass and shelf filters to simulate the formants of the exhaust, airbox, and tailpipe, resonating at specific frequencies with gentle Q. This model naturally blooms harmonics as RPM changes. The goal is to make the sound accurate to the physics.\n\n\nTurbocharger modeling integrates a compressor blade-passing whine and turbine whistle, sweeping between 2600–5400 Hz correlated with throttle and RPM, including lagged boost and wastegate flutter. An MGU-K gear whine proportional to wheel speed is also included, providing a subtle hybrid character.\n\nAerodynamic wind rush utilizes dual-channel stereo noise, filtered across various frequency bands, with amplitude scaling to the square of velocity, most prominent in onboard and fast-moving camera angles. Kerb and tire load rumble is also added.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"run_command","args":{"CommandLine":"@'\nimport json, math\nimport numpy as np\n\nwith open(r'D:\\项目session\\blender-supercar\\track_data\\silverstone_track.json', encoding='utf-8') as f:\n    data = json.load(f)\nP = data['points']\nLAP_S = data['meta']['sim_lap_s']\nDT = LAP_S / 2160.0\n\nts = np.array([p['t'] for p in P])\nvs = np.array([p['v_mps'] for p in P])\nks = np.array([abs(p['curv_1pm'] or 0.0) for p in P])\n\nt_frames = np.arange(2161) * DT\nt_frames[-1] = min(t_frames[-1], ts[-1] - 1e-6)\nv_f = np.interp(t_frames, ts, vs)\nk_f = np.interp(t_frames, ts, ks)\nv_kmh = v_f * 3.6\nacc = np.gradient(v_f, DT)\n\n# Proposed 8-speed v_top at 12550 RPM\nv_top = np.array([88.0, 110.0, 135.3, 165.1, 199.7, 239.7, 285.2, 336.5])\nup_kmh = v_top[:-1] * (12380.0 / 12550.0)\ndn_kmh = up_kmh - 5.5\n\ngear = np.zeros(2161, dtype=int)\ng = 7 # start at ~297.5 km/h -> 8th gear (0-indexed 7)\nlast_shift_t = -1.0\nupshifts, downshifts = 0, 0\nfor i in range(2161):\n    t = i * DT\n    vk = v_kmh[i]\n    if t - last_shift_t >= 0.13:\n        if g < 7 and vk > up_kmh[g]:\n            g += 1\n            upshifts += 1\n            last_shift_t = t\n        elif g > 0 and vk < dn_kmh[g - 1]:\n            g -= 1\n            downshifts += 1\n            last_shift_t = t\n    gear[i] = g + 1\n\nrpm = np.clip(v_kmh / v_top[gear - 1] * 12550.0, 6800.0, 12650.0)\nprint(f\"Total upshifts: {upshifts}, downshifts: {downshifts}\")\nprint(f\"Gears visited: { sorted(set(gear.tolist())) }\")\nprint(f\"RPM min={rpm.min():.0f}, p05={np.percentile(rpm,5):.0f}, median={np.median(rpm):.0f}, p95={np.percentile(rpm,95):.0f}, max={rpm.max():.0f}\")\nprint(f\"RPM when v>=80 km/h: min={rpm[v_kmh>=80].min():.0f}, max={rpm[v_kmh>=80].max():.0f}\")\nprint(f\"Accel min={acc.min():.1f} m/s^2, max={acc.max():.1f} m/s^2\")\nprint(f\"Frames with |curv|>0.008: {(k_f > 0.008).sum()} / 2161 ({100*(k_f > 0.008).mean():.1f}%), max curv={k_f.max():.4f}\")\n'@ | python -","WaitMsBeforeAsync":5000,"toolSummary":"Verify 8-speed gearbox RPM and kerb curvature stats"}}]}
