reo panning direction was reversed; the logic now correctly accounts for the camera's location relative to the track, ensuring proper Doppler shift and localization. The updated calculation uses a vector cross-product and incorporates the camera's trackside location and viewing direction. This updated method accurately maps interaural time differences for realistic stereo panning.\n\n\nThe acoustic model integrates camera-relative sound panning, accounting for Doppler shift and distance. Chase and orbit shots now feature a balanced mix of exhaust, wastegate pops, and intake whistle, with stereo panning that follows the car's offset from the camera's perspective. Wheel shots incorporate tire scrub, kerb rumble, and mechanical sounds, panned toward the car.\n\n\nAir absorption modeling now includes high-altitude effects, applying a lowpass filter scaled with camera altitude, along with near-field proximity reduction. Stereo open-air track reverb is widened with specified reflection timings, and distance attenuation is smoothed with carefully calculated fades. A uniform 50 ms equal-power crossfade is implemented at every shot cut boundary, using cosine and sine windows to create a click-free transition.\n\n\nThe crankshaft phase across all co-moving shots is phase-aligned due to the radial velocity being approximately zero in those views. This means that engine phase is sample-synchronous across the 50 ms crossfade, preventing comb filtering or phasing dips during shot cuts. Calculating the phase offset for each shot's crankshaft is critical for ensuring coherent engine harmonics.\n\n\nA 50 ms equal-power crossfade is now implemented, ensuring a seamless acoustic perspective shift without phase cancellation. Instantaneous physical Doppler frequency is calculated at every sample for fixed cameras, using a Doppler scale factor. This is confirmed by deriving the time derivative of the crankshaft phase equation. Further, direct integration of the derived equation over the shot slice is also being confirmed.\n\n\nThe crankshaft phase calculation for the co-moving shots is now simplified to a two-line vectorized NumPy operation, leveraging the fact that radial velocity is nearly zero. A new script, `pipeline/synth_sfx.py`, will be written to synthesize the sound effects, ensuring that it is self-documenting, clean, and fast. The CLI is designed for direct execution, reading track data and animation scripts, and producing a WAV file with detailed telemetry and audio QA statistics.\n\n\nThe brief outlines specific requirements for soundtrack mixing, including balancing music and sound effects while avoiding clipping. The existing scripts need updating to produce silent, music-only, and mixed audio versions. The immediate goal is to generate the specified mixed MP4 files, verifying streams, durations, and loudness.\n\n\nThe sound synthesis script now outputs the engine SFX WAV file with precise normalization and fade parameters. A decision point arises: should the audio mix be performed in the existing script or via a separate process?